course: dictionary
lesson: 11195
title: One small word, many jobs
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: «With» is a tiny word that does lots of jobs. Its most common job is to show who or what goes along with something, as in «I went with Dad». When you meet «with», look at the words around it and ask which meaning fits.
explain: «With» often tells you what something has in or on it: «a girl with red hair», «a box with 6 eggs». It can also tell you what you use to do something: «cut with scissors». The words after «with» are your clue.
explain: «With» can mean because of, as in «shaking with cold», or against, as in «argue with». The feeling of the sentence helps: an argument is not a friendly walk, so «argue with» means against someone.
fact: «With» is a preposition: it links a noun or pronoun to the rest of the sentence.
fact: «With» means together with someone: «I went with Dad».
fact: «With» means having something: «a box with 6 eggs» is a box that has 6 eggs in it.
fact: «With» can tell you what you use: «cut with scissors».
fact: «With» can mean because of, as in «shaking with cold», or against, as in «fight with».
exercise: «I went with Dad to the shop.» What does with mean here? | Without | Against | *Together with | Far away from
exercise: «Cut the paper with scissors.» What does with tell you? | Who did the job | How the paper feels | Where the paper is kept | *What you use to do the job
exercise: «Shaking with cold, the puppy hid under the bed.» What does with mean here? | Together with | *Because of | Against | Having
test: «She argued with her brother about the game.» What does with mean here? | Near her brother | Given to her brother | *Against her brother | Together with her brother
test: «The box with 6 eggs in it is on the table.» What does with mean here? | *Having | Against | Using | Without
exam: In a formal text, which phrase means the same as «together with»? | away from | *in conjunction with | instead of | despite
